welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and our guest  is Sarah Schneiderman an assistant  professor of anthropology at Yale  University professor Schneiderman is a  socio-cultural anthropologist working in  the Himalayan regions of Nepal India and  China especially the Tibetan Autonomous  Region her research addresses the  relationships between political  discourse ritual practice cultural  performance and cross-border migration  in producing contemporary ethnic  identities since 2009 she has been  involved in a project entitled  inequality and affirmative action in  South Asia current experiences and  future agendas in India and Nepal today  we talk with her about that project and  her research on inequality and  affirmative action  welcome professor Schneiderman thank you  let's begin with an overview of the  project tell us a little bit about it  okay well it came together as a  partnership project between several  junior scholars we were all based in the  UK at the time because I completed my  postdoc at Cambridge University just  before coming to Yale so several of us  based in the UK and then several  scholars in India and Nepal and all of  us were interested in the policies of  affirmative action but more importantly  what its results look like on the ground  and lived reality many of us are  anthropologists there are six of us  involved in the project and one is a  historian one is a political scientist  and one focuses on education but all of  us wanted to look in a more fine-grained  manner at how people on the ground in  various communities understood the  policies that were being formulated and  then reacted to them in terms of their  own cultural practice in everyday life  so we got together and put in an  application to the British Academy to  have a three year set of research  research initiatives in which we would  look at both India and Nepal  comparatively speaking  to understand how the history of what  are often called reservations in India  that's but a system that's been in place  since the 1950s and it's one of the  oldest and most robust systems of  affirmative action anywhere in the world  what we wanted to understand was the  implications of that history for current  debates in Nepal which is currently in a  moment of state restructuring there's  been a ten-year long conflict which  officially ended in 2006 between the  state and Maoist insurgents and in the  wake of that post-conflict settlement a  new Constituent Assembly was elected and  one of the major agendas of that new  governing body in Nepal has been to  develop the country's first-ever system  of affirmative action because Nepal is  located adjacent to India and has a  great deal of cross-border historical  connections and also contemporary flows  of people and ideas and so forth it  seemed like a natural thing to do to  bring the conversations which have been  ongoing in India for a long time about  these issues into the discursive  environment in Nepal of course some of  that is already happening at the policy  level but what we wanted to do was to  bring a rigorous scholarly perspective  to bear on some of these two policy  debates and particularly an ethnographic  perspective so one like I said at the  beginning that would look really in  detail at how people were experiencing  policy and how they were transforming  their lives in relation to it and then  how in effect they might also request  the state to transfer form the policies  for them alright before we go any  further let's define what you are  calling affirmative action mm-hmm okay  well this is a term which can often be  very controversial and it's taken to  mean different things in different parts  of the world in India it's largely been  discussed as equivalent with positive  discrimination so basically any measure  which all other things being equal gives  special benefits to people of certain  recognized groups because they're  perceived to have a past of  discrimination and not equal access to  often government resources jobs  education and so forth  so affirmative action actually refers to  a broad range of possible initiatives  some of which can be positive  discrimination as I just just described  there's also a concept of positive  action which  is more prevalently used in the UK which  does not actually include specific  benefits but it engenders it aims to  make available information to people  from a wider range of different groups  so there are many different forms of  these affirmative action measures I know  the project is wrapping up in 2012  they're having their final conference in  July are there any particular goals that  you would like to have seen the project  reach at that point well our major goal  has been to create a body of literature  on this material that's what been one  goal another goal has been to actually  bring this scholarly material into play  and the policy arena and so we see some  of that happening we currently have  three different publications in process  resulting from the work in the project  and I should say what some of that work  has comprised has been research projects  mostly carried out by graduate students  in India and Nepal funded through this  project and supervised by myself and the  other members of the partnership in the  UK India and Nepal and so they've  conducted case studies in different  parts of both countries looking at these  micro level dynamics so some of that  work will be published in an edited  volume in Nepal some of it will be  published in a special issue of an  anthropology journal which myself and my  co-editor and project organiser  Albus Shah are editing she's at  Goldsmiths College in London and then in  the summer conference in Kathmandu we'll  be bringing together scholars from all  over the world who have worked on these  dynamics both to talk with each other to  see what's unique about the Nepal  scenario which can yield insights for  scholarship more broadly defined but  also what kinds of insights those  scholars can contribute to the dynamics  in Nepal and that conference will also  include meetings between the scholars  and Nepali Constituent Assembly members  and other policymakers involved in the  top level discussions there have you  done any comparative analyses between  Nepal for instance and perhaps the  United States or any other countries  this is something which is of great  interest to me and is a direction that  my own work might go in I'm actually  teaching a class  here at Yale in the spring which is  titled affirmative action in South Asia  and the US and so through that class  I'll be beginning to look at some of  that literature of course the  comparative project is really important  and there have been scholars who have  looked at particularly the example of  India in relation to what it might offer  to the US but most of that analysis has  come from quantitative perspective so  from sociology political science  economics looking at the numbers does  affirmative action action actually  increase people's socio-economic  standing but what I'm more interested in  looking at is how it actually affects  people's sense of identity and their  cultural practices and so that's a  perspective that we've been able to  develop in the South Asian context which  I also think it would be really useful  to apply here in the US okay I know you  believe that there should be affirmative  action especially in Nepal can you kind  of extrapolate on that a little bit why  you feel that way again this is a very  controversial issue and I want to make  clear that I'm not in favor of  affirmative action measures that are  applied in a sort of haphazard manner  rather I think that careful research is  necessary to understand what the  underlying social and cultural dynamics  are and that's of course what we're  trying to do through this project and  then applying that knowledge part of the  issue in both Nepal in India has to do  with histories of classification how  people are actually classified so that  they fall into categories that could be  beneficiaries of affirmative action in  India some of the categories are called  scheduled tribes and as our st so people  who come from ethnic communities or a  caste I mean I know that the caste  system is still in place in India so  that I would imagine is as part of those  tribal groups you know so there are two  separate groups scheduled tribes are one  legally recognized set of groups and  there's many many groups within that  large scale designation and then  scheduled castes are the other group and  those are communities who historically  would have been at the lower rungs of  the Hindu caste system so otherwise  known as Untouchables or adul it's then  there's another set of groups which the  Indian state  more recently recognized called other  backwards classes and that is the  official legal term so what happens  through all of these processes of state  recognition is that people actually have  to prove their eligibility for belonging  to one of these groups and that leads to  all kinds of cultural politics that can  be very difficult and divisive now in  Nepal the same sorts of groups exist in  the sense that there was a caste system  which is officially illegal but still  continues in practice in the sense that  people do discriminate against others on  the basis of caste and there are also  many ethnic minorities who do not fall  within that caste system and because of  this stratification which was also  legally recognized by the state in legal  codes going back even to the 1850s  basically people have not had equal  access to education and resources over  time and in Nepal it's only been after  1990 that it's actually been legal to  talk about ethnic difference before 1990  when the country returned to democracy  you couldn't even use an ethnic name or  an ethnic language in any kind of public  domain and due to that history of  discrimination and marginalization  people feel very strongly that there  need to be some sort of corrective  measures to that just to give you a few  statistics for instance in Nepal in the  civil service so this is 2010 data the  Brahmin group which is the top level of  the caste system is about 12% of the  population but they have over 70% of the  top level civil service jobs by contrast  the ethnic minorities are about 30% of  the population but they have only around  1% of the civil service jobs so the  inequalities are very vast and in order  to bring that sort of inequality into  some sort of balance within a relatively  short time frame during the process of  state restructuring that's going on now  in Nepal many people feel that it is  necessary to have these kinds of  measures and I support that really on  the basis of my ethnographic work with  people from marginalized communities who  express those sorts of desires to me it  seems like it would be a very  complicated policy to put forth I mean  so what is the current situation now in  Nepal for instance  yes it is very complicated and of course  that's a term that scholars like to use  a lot and so the question is how we can  distill some of that complication into  something that's actually practicable at  a policy level in Nepal there has been a  Constituent Assembly so basically a  governing body which is at once meant to  govern but also to draft a new  constitution that has been in place  since 2008 after elections were called  for the first time in over 10 years and  they were supposed to draft a  constitution within two years but they  missed that deadline then it was three  years they missed that deadline and now  they're continuing to work on that and  one of the main divisive issues is this  question of affirmative action how to  actually set it up in a way that occurs  people who were discriminated against in  the past a fair chance without creating  new forms of discrimination against  members of the previously dominant group  so this is a subject a very active  debate and it's in tandem with the  discussion about state restructuring so  Nepal was a unitary state in the sense  that it had no States or federal  structure like we have here in the u.s.  it was just one homogeneous polity and  now the idea is to break it down into  fourteen different provinces each of  which might in fact be governed by an  ethnic my Nick group that is dominant in  that area but as you can imagine this is  also very controversial and so the  question is how can equality actually be  assured in a way that doesn't actually  fragment the state and also makes people  feel like they're all getting equal  access to the limited resources the  state has to offer I mean it's something  that you can probably debate and talk  about forever but it gets to a point  where you have to stop and say okay  right here's what we're going to do so  that you have not reached that point yet  and Nepal no I mean of course many  people have their own ideas about what  should happen but it still has not been  actually formulated in in any kind of  legal manner those Constituent Assembly  debates are ongoing and as a point of  comparison in India the Constituent  Assembly debates had a similar form but  were held between 1948 and 1950 and  India's affirmative action system  emerges out of those debates so what  happened in India 50 years ago is  happening in Nepal now and that's why  the comparison between the two countries  and taking lessons learn from India's  fifty years also about the negative  sides of affirmative action and what  might Nepal might do to avoid some of  the problems that India has encountered  that's a very valuable what exercise  what are some of the challenges that  India has encountered um well one of the  one that I look at most carefully in my  own ethnographic work is it relates back  to this issue of classification that I  mentioned earlier so basically what are  the criteria out there for a group to be  recognized as a scheduled tribe well in  India the list dates to 1965 and it  includes evidence of being primitive and  these are the Indian government's terms  not mine geographical isolation  backwards Ness their own traditional  culture and some sort of oral tradition  so they're very normative terms that  basically say people should have this  kind of cultural practice if they are to  be recognized as tribal and so what that  has compelled as a whole set of cultural  performances by which people try to  match that criteria by outperforming  each other as evermore tribal so people  who may on a normal day wear a shirt and  pants and work in an office in front of  a computer when it comes time to  actually put on a performance for  members of the state who may be part of  the Commission which would verify their  community as a tribal group they'll put  on what they perceived to be tribal  costumes often creating things which  never previously existed and so there's  a kind of fabrication of culture that  goes on which in itself is not  necessarily negative but it begs the  question of what actually are the  criteria for recognition and why do  people actually have to manipulate their  daily reality in order to gain benefits  which really should be tagged to  economic inequality not the performance  of certain cultural traits and so this  is a problem that's now widely  recognized in India but the question is  how do you transform those legal codes  at this particular moment whereas Nepal  has the opportunity not to reify culture  as a criteria but instead to find ways  of evaluating economic inequality and  the ways  it's linked to cast an ethnicity and a  much more nuanced manner and there are  various groups that are actively trying  to come up with those definitional  rubrics and what do you think the future  holds I mean how far down the line how  many years perhaps do you think it will  take before something is instituted in  terms of affirmative action particularly  in Nepal  well we'll have to see they now actually  have a deadline for the Constitution  hold on it's September August at the end  of November but it remains to be seen  whether that will actually come to pass  or not the very sticky issue in Nepal  again is this issue of federal  restructuring on the basis of ethnicity  so should there be new provinces or  states which are delineated along ethnic  residence patterns and as you can  imagine that's a very controversial  issue so will that happen or not we  still don't know my hope is that they  will find a way to institute affirmative  action at a central state level so that  these powers are not devolved to newly  formed States but rather are  administered from whatever remains of  the central state so that such policies  can be formulated in a way that are  equitable for people living all across  the country regardless of where they are  actually having new states with new  administrative structures to set up  responsible for developing affirmative  action policy at the same time seems  like a great challenge to me yes I agree  well thank you so much for being here  with us today and sharing some of your  work thank you very much for more  information about Professor Schneiderman  and her research please visit our  website at Yale edu backslash Macmillan  report be sure to join us again for  another episode of the Macmillan report  made possible through funding from the  Whitney and Betty Macmillan Center for  International and area studies at Yale

